{"name":"Nine Iron","hex":"#46434a","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"nine","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["NATO & ICAO radiotelephony clear code (spelling-alphabet name) for the digit 9."]},{"part":"Numeral","definitions":["A numerical value equal to 9; the number following eight and preceding ten.","Describing a group or set with nine elements."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nine"},{"term":"iron","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A common, inexpensive metal, silvery grey when untarnished, that rusts, is attracted by magnets, and is used in making steel: a chemical element having atomic number 26 and symbol Fe.","Any material, not a steel, predominantly made of elemental iron."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of the metal iron.","Strong , inflexible."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/iron"}],"entries":[{"title":"Iron (golf)","blocks":[{"text":"An iron is a type of club used in the sport of golf to propel the ball towards the hole. Irons typically have shorter shafts and smaller clubheads than woods, the head is made of solid iron or steel, and the head's primary feature is a large, flat, angled face, usually scored with grooves. Irons are used in a wide variety of situations, typically from the teeing ground on many par-3 holes, from the fairway or rough as the player approaches the green, and to extract the ball from hazards, such as bunkers or even shallow water hazards."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Golf_clubs.jpg/330px-Golf_clubs.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iron_(golf)"},{"title":"Iron","blocks":[{"text":"Iron is a chemical element; it has symbol Fe and atomic number 26. It is a metal that belongs to the first transition series and group 8 of the periodic table. It is, by mass, the most common element on Earth, forming much of Earth's outer and inner core. It is the fourth most abundant element in the Earth's crust. In its metallic state it was mainly deposited by meteorites."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Iron_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg/330px-Iron_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iron"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Golf_clubs.jpg/500px-Golf_clubs.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Golf_clubs.jpg/500px-Golf_clubs.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Irons in a golf bag","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGolf_clubs.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Photo_of_a_cavity_back_style_iron.png/330px-Photo_of_a_cavity_back_style_iron.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Photo_of_a_cavity_back_style_iron.png/330px-Photo_of_a_cavity_back_style_iron.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cavity back style iron","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APhoto_of_a_cavity_back_style_iron.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Iron_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg/500px-Iron_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Iron_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg/1280px-Iron_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Iron_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg/500px-Iron_electrolytic_and_1cm3_cube.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&u